                    <span class="profile-eyebrow">A Steady Flame</span>
                    <h2>Devotion that became lifelong responsibility</h2>
                    <p>Maa Basanti’s life joined inner discipline with meticulous service. Her quiet strength ensured that Bhaiyaji’s spiritual knowledge remained living, accessible and inseparable from human welfare.</p>
                </header>

                <article class="profile-chapter">
                    <div><span class="profile-chapter-number">Chapter I</span><h2>Embracing the path of Sadhna</h2></div>
                    <div class="profile-chapter-copy">
                        <p>Born into the respected and well-educated family of Advocate Shri Harkalalji Manihar and Smt. Sirekanwarji, Basanti Manihar completed her M.A. and B.Ed. and was known from childhood for simplicity, virtue and concern for others.</p>
                        <p>In 1964, she came into spiritual contact with her maternal cousin, Bhaiyaji Shri Nandkishore Sharda. After deep reflection and demanding spiritual tests, she chose to walk under his guidance. Renouncing material comfort, she devoted herself to Jagatjanani Maa and received Bhaiyaji’s spiritual knowledge through disciplined experience.</p>
                        <p>For many years, Bhaiyaji, Maa Basanti and Madhu Maa practised together at Maniharon Ki Haveli in Jodhpur. In 1995, their Sadhna continued at the newly completed Manidweep—a home that would become the spiritual centre of their shared work.</p>
                    </div>
                </article>

                <article class="profile-chapter">
                    <div><span class="profile-chapter-number">Chapter II</span><h2>Carrying forward the sacred mission</h2></div>
                    <div class="profile-chapter-copy">
                        <p>Maa Basanti was the Founder of the Swami Vivekanand Students’ Welfare Charitable Trust, established on 10 June 1996. After Bhaiyaji left his mortal form in 2003, she accepted responsibility for the work he had begun and guided every trust with care, precision and spiritual fidelity.</p>
                        <p>She held two resolutions close: to make Bhaiyaji’s simplified divine knowledge available for the welfare of all, and to continue performing Satkarma throughout her life. She fulfilled both through the publication of important spiritual books, the expansion of educational service and a constant personal presence among seekers and students.</p>
                    </div>
                </article>

                <div class="profile-reflection">Preserve the wisdom in its original spirit, and let every act of service become its living expression.</div>

                <article class="profile-chapter">
                    <div><span class="profile-chapter-number">Chapter III</span><h2>Guidance for the whole person</h2></div>
                    <div class="profile-chapter-copy">
                        <p>Through Sunday morning sessions, Maa Basanti nurtured confidence, devotion, positive thinking and moral strength among young women. Education became a means of forming sensitive, self-respecting citizens rather than merely acquiring qualifications.</p>
                        <p>In Sunday evening gatherings, she helped householders understand Bhaiyaji’s Buddhi-Vivek Yog Sadhna and find balance between material responsibilities and spiritual progress. Her guidance addressed life’s difficulties practically, enabling seekers to cultivate steadiness, harmony and inner joy within family life.</p>
                        <p>Maa Basanti left her mortal form on 7 May 2021. Her published work, the institutions she strengthened and the countless lives she guided remain a luminous continuation of her presence.</p>
                    </div>
                </article>
